# =============================================================================
# Benchmarks: read_plan
# =============================================================================
#
# Measures get_many cold-cache under three policies, interleaved per pass:
#   plain    - independent reads (ReadPlanPolicy::disabled)
#   random   - MADV_RANDOM on the mapping, no planning (readahead suppressed)
#   prefetch - merged extents + madvise(MADV_WILLNEED)
# It refuses to run on tmpfs/ramfs/devtmpfs, so MTXDB_BENCH_ROOT must point at
# a real disk. Run the same sweep on both an HDD and an SSD: the verdict is
# device-dependent (see below).
#
# Measured result (1M records x 1 KiB, cold, careful HDD vs SSD):
#   random  is the portable win: 4.5-6.5x vs plain on SSD, 1.4-3.4x on sparse
#           HDD, ~1.0 on dense HDD.
#   prefetch only wins on HDD at target spacing >= ~700 KiB (1.9-3x vs random),
#           is neutral by ~174 KiB, and LOSES on SSD (0.6-0.85x vs random).
#   The merge gap barely matters from 0 to ~256 KiB; the shipped 64 KiB sits in
#   that flat, good regime.
# So do not enable prefetch by default anywhere. random is the better opt-in,
# but it is not yet a safe *default*: see the warm/unmeasured caveat below.
#
# Warm-cache run (MTXDB_READ_PLAN_WARM=1, data resident): random vs plain was
# 0.94-1.20x at 1-5 ms, i.e. within the pass spread - no regression at this
# granularity, but not proof random is faster warm. prefetch was 0.75-0.93x
# warm (needless WILLNEED), reinforcing that it stays opt-in. Only sparse
# POINT reads were tested warm: no dense case, and no sequential scan or
# compaction. That last case is the one that decides whether MADV_RANDOM can
# be a default, because it is persistent mapping state (it is never reset, so
# it also affects later scans/compaction on the same mapping) and suppression
# would remove the readahead that makes scans fast. All measurements are one
# rotational HDD and one cheap SATA SSD; NVMe and other drives are untested.
#
# Reading a run: check the printed "target density (sparse)" line first - the
# median gap must be well above the merge gap for a run to say anything about
# spacing. Then check the cold guard passed (the sparse `plain` row read at
# least half a page per target off the device; the printed fraction of the
# store is near 1.0 for a full scan, or warns if above 2x). Trust a ratio only
# when the sparse `plain` and `prefetch` (or `random`) rows differ.
#
# Sweep knobs (each has a built-in default; override to probe):
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_RECORDS=1000000   # record count (~1 GB at 1 KiB)
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_TARGETS=500       # spacing ~2 MiB; 1000 -> ~700 KiB
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_PAYLOAD=1024      # bytes per record payload
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_PASSES=3          # median over N passes
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_DENSE=0           # skip the dense rows (halves runtime)
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_WARM=1            # measure with data resident (no eviction)
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_REPACK=1          # repack (scan/compaction) regression check
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_GAP=65536         # merge_gap_bytes (try 0..262144)
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_EXTENT=8388608    # max_extent_bytes
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_MIN=16            # min_batch_candidates
#
# Ingest append-policy A/B (separate from the read sweeps; INGEST_ONLY stops
# after ingest so only the append path is timed):
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_APPEND=buffered    # one pwrite per ~1 MiB vs eager per record
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_INGEST_ONLY=1      # skip the read-plan sweep after ingest
#
# Pauses between reads so the page cache can be dropped by hand (as root):
#   sudo sh -c 'echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches'
# Without this, read_plan falls back to `vmtouch -e`, which only evicts clean
# pages of the named files and can leave the read warm. Left off by default so
# a root run uses the in-process drop_page_caches() instead.
# export MTXDB_READ_PLAN_MANUAL_DROP=1


